# 208: "A function with no `return` returns Nil" (§2), displayed as `nil` by the
# one `Display for Value` (§3). Three shapes that all fall off the end: an empty
# body, a body that ends in a statement, and a body whose `if` was not taken.
# Note the interleaving — `prints` emits its own line before the outer `print`
# appends `nil`, because a `print` appends its line only after every argument
# has been evaluated (§6 `.33`).
--- source
fn empty() { }
fn prints() { print "side effect"; }
fn skipped(n) { if n > 0 { return "positive"; } }
print empty();
print prints();
print skipped(1);
print skipped(-1);
--- expect
nil
side effect
nil
positive
nil
